minal). Actually terminals are 1 and 9. I have none in hand. My melds include W9 (terminal) and B8 (not terminal). So I have some terminal tiles in melds. Possible patterns:  \n- Half Flush? My melds are mixed suits: B and W, hand has T and B and W. Not one suit.  \n- All Chows? Possible if I complete another chow.  \n- All Simples? My hand has no terminals or honors, so yes if I avoid them. But my melds include W9 (terminal), so not All Simples.  \n- Mixed Triple Chow? Not.  \n- Short Straight? My melds are [B6,B7,B8] and [W7,W8,W9], they are both chows but not in same suit.  \nGiven this, I probably want to aim for a quick win with low points, but need at least 8 points. Currently, my hand might not reach 8 points unless I get some patterns like: All Chows (2), Self Drawn (1), etc. I need to check what patterns I can get.  \nIf I complete a hand with: three chows (two already melded, one more from hand) and a pair, with all simples? But my melds have W9 (terminal), so not all simples. Maybe add Pung of simples?  \nBetter to think about discarding: I need to keep tiles that can form melds quickly.  \nMy hand:  \nW2 (good for pair or pung)  \nT2, T3 (can form chow with T1 or T4)  \nT8 (needs T6,T7 or T7,T9 for chow)  \nB3 (good for pair or pung)  \nB7 (lonely)  \nSo worst tiles: B7 (only one, no immediate connections), T8 (only one, needs specific tiles), maybe T2/T3 are better kept together.  \nPotential discards: B7 or T8.  \nAlso, I have two W2, keeping them gives chance for pung. Two B3 similar.  \nValid actions: Play W2; Play T3; Play T2; Play B7; Play T8; Play B3.  \nIf I discard B7, I keep T2,T3 for chow possibility, and keep pairs.  \nIf I discard T8, similar.  \nIf I discard T2 or T3, I break the potential chow.  \nIf I discard W2 or B3, I break a pair.  \nSo best is discard B7 or T8. Between them, B7 has only 1 left in unseen tiles (2 total, I have 1 in hand, and meld used one? Wait, unseen tiles list says B7:2. That means total 4 copies? Actually, the list shows counts of remaining tiles in the wall? The \"Unshown tiles\" list shows numbers like B7:2. That means there are 2 B7 tiles left unseen (including in wall and other players' hands). Since I have 1 B7 in hand, and my meld used 1 B7, that's 2 accounted for, so total 4, yes. So 2 left. But still, B7 is less useful than T8? T8 has only 1 left (T8:2 total, I have 1, so 1 left). So T8 is rarer.  \nBut from opponent history, many tiles have been discarded. Looking at history: Previous player played T8, W8, etc. So T8 has been discarded once? Actually history: previous player played T8 (in \"Play T8\"). So one T8 is out. That means the remaining T8 might be safe to discard? But I might need it for chow.  \nGiven I have T2,T3, it's easier to complete a chow with T1 or T4 (more tiles available: T1 has 1 left, T4 has 4 left). For T8 chow, I need T6,T7 or T7,T9. T6 has 3 left, T7 has 3 left, T9 has 3 left. So possible.  \nBut discarding B7 seems safest because it's less connected to my other tiles.  \nAlso, considering potential points: If I win with a hand containing a pung of W2 or B3, I could get Pung of Simples (1 point each) but not enough. I need more patterns. Maybe aim for All Chows (2) + Self Drawn (1) + maybe Last Tile or something? Not sure.  \nGiven the game state, I should discard the tile that is least useful and safest.  \nB7 is a simple tile, not many out.  \nI'll discard B7.  \n\nReason: B7 is isolated in my hand with no immediate meld potential, while keeping T2 and T3 together offers a better chance to complete a chow. Discarding B7 retains two potential pairs (W2 and B3) and maintains flexibility to pursue either a pung or chow-based hand.  \n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
